    "narrative": "For tonight\u2019s slate, **Seiya Suzuki** (66.29) and **Tyler Soderstrom** (66.15) lead the charge as our strongest plays. **Seiya Suzuki** benefits from a highly favorable matchup against Springs, where he exploits a generous park factor and a solid order position, while **Tyler Soderstrom** remains a locked-in start against Rea, bolstered by strong SP skill indicators and his high-leverage cleanup spot. **Austin Riley** (64.19) is also worth highlighting; while his base projection is moderate, he is elevated by an elite BvP history against Corbin, proving that even against a lefty, he is a primary driver for our RBI production today.\n\nManaging our scarce SB assets remains a primary focus. **Corbin Carroll** (60.20) survives a brutal matchup against Ohtani primarily because his elite sprint speed makes him a category-definer, regardless of the SP's dominance. Conversely, **Jose Caballero** (48.34) faces downward pressure due to the opposing catcher\u2019s elite pop time, forcing a lower efficiency rating despite his speed profile. Our most difficult decision on the bench was **Kyle Karros** (49.69); while he presents a better raw output than **Alex Freeland**, **Alex Freeland**\u2019s position as a primary middle infielder and the specific lineup construction needs kept him in the starting slot despite the challenging matchup against Gallen.\n\nRisks today are dominated by the presence of Ohtani on the mound. Both **Gabriel Moreno** (47.53) and **Vinnie Pasquantino** (54.26) have their efficiency scores significantly suppressed by his presence, serving as a reminder that we are prioritizing lineup stability over chasing ceiling against top-tier arms. We continue to monitor the IL status of **Trevor Story** and **Mickey Moniak**, while keeping an eye on **James Wood** (58.86); his high xwOBA keeps him as a near-elite play today, and he remains our most vital anchor for power and speed production out of the leadoff spot.",
